224: \]
225: All coefficients are integral, and \(M^2=I\). Relabelling
226: the selected edge gives the two other matrices recorded in
227: the exact certificate.
228: 
229: ## 3. The actual relative return and its variation
230: 
231: Use a fan retaining the physical delta edge and the actual
232: extending cusp product of K27. The positive parameter return
233: has a delta-equivariant representative \(F\) which is the
234: identity on a neighbourhood of the entire three-curve union.
235: To obtain it, take the closed Cox product on the cusp and
236: equivariant product connections on the remainder; their
237: partition-of-unity interpolation is still equivariant and is
238: fixed on the chosen cusp collar. This is the same physical
239: return, with its actual parameter closing.
240: 
241: The ordinary toric isomorphism, the common blowup charts and
242: the bounded Cox maps all intertwine the parameter products.
243: Hence a flop leaves this actual map \(F\) on the common
244: exterior unchanged. In particular no new relative boundary
245: framing is substituted in (P3K.29.6).
246: 
247: Since \(F\) is the identity near each \(C_i\), the marked
248: dual-cell generators show that it is the identity on
249: \(H_3(E_C)\) and \(H_4(E_C)\). Define its integral variation by
250: \[
251:  \operatorname{Var}_F:H_3(E_C,\partial E_C)\longrightarrow
252:  H_3(E_C),\qquad [D]\longmapsto[F(D)-D].
253:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.8}
254: \]
255: Let \(V\) be its matrix, with absolute basis \(m_i\) and
256: relative basis \(D_i\).
257: 
258: This matrix is symmetric. For a direct proof, double \(E_C\)
259: and extend \(F\) by the identity on the second half. The
260: absolute meridians and the doubles of \(D_i\) form the two
261: primitive middle-dimensional groups of the double. Their
262: intersection matrix has \(m_i\cdot D_j=\delta_{ij}\) and
263: \(D_j\cdot m_i=-\delta_{ij}\). The extended map fixes all
264: \(m_i\) and takes \(D_j\) to \(D_j+\sum_iV_{ij}m_i\).
265: Preservation of the intersections of two \(D\)'s gives
266: \(-V_{ij}+V_{ji}=0\). This proof does not require a choice
267: of a Lagrangian among the doubled \(D\)'s.
268: 
269: ## 4. The circle-trace kernel is an integral primitive lattice
270: 
271: The delta action is free on \(E_C\), since all its fixed points
272: are in the removed fixed curve \(C_0\). Put
273: \(B_C=E_C/S^1_\delta\). In the actual orbit \(Y=S^5\), the
274: image of \(C\) is the fixed two-sphere together with two
275: embedded arcs joining its two marked points. The two
276: nonfixed curves have free circle orbits away from their
277: endpoints, so their quotients are precisely these arcs.
278: The local Hopf chart supplies the regular-neighbourhood
279: map at the endpoints. Thus \(B_C\) is the complement of a
280: regular neighbourhood of this marked two-sphere-and-arcs
281: space in \(S^5\).
282: 
283: The same dual-cell construction gives
284: \[
285:  H_2(B_C)=\mathbb Z,\qquad H_3(B_C)=\mathbb Z^2,\qquad
286:  H_4(B_C)=0.
287:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.9}
288: \]
289: Both \(B_C\) and \(E_C\) are simply connected. The Euler
290: class \(x\) of the actual circle bundle is the primitive
291: generator of \(H^2(B_C)\). Its restriction to a meridian
292: two-sphere of the fixed curve is the original unit Hopf
293: bundle, with its given physical orientation.
294: 
295: Write \(\Delta\) for the circle-trace operation on relative
296: chains:
297: \[
298:  \Delta:H_2(E_C,\partial E_C)\longrightarrow
299:           H_3(E_C,\partial E_C).
300:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.10}
301: \]
302: It is induced by the actual action map
303: \(S^1\times E_C\to E_C\), with the positive delta circle.
304: The trace operation and the two Poincare--Lefschetz maps
305: identify (P3K.29.10), up to a single overall orientation
306: sign, with
307: \[
308:  H^4(E_C)\xrightarrow{\pi_!}H^3(B_C)
309:                     \xrightarrow{\pi^*}H^3(E_C).
310:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.11}
311: \]
312: One can see this equality directly on a transverse dual
313: chain: tracing its intersection once around the fibre is
314: the pullback of its oriented fibre integration. The
315: possibly opposite common sign does not change the image.
316: 
317: The integral Gysin sequence and (P3K.29.9) show that
318: \(\pi_!\) in (P3K.29.11) is an isomorphism. The pullback
319: \(\pi^*\) is injective. Its image is exactly the primitive
320: lattice of cohomology classes dual to \(m_1,m_2\).
321: Indeed \(m_0\) maps by the Hopf fibration to a two-sphere
322: and therefore has zero image in \(H_3(B_C)\). At an
323: interior point of either nonfixed curve, the quotient of
324: its circle tangent is a point, and its complex normal
325: four-disc maps diffeomorphically to a transverse normal
326: four-disc of the corresponding arc. Hence its meridian
327: maps, with degree of absolute value one, to the marked
328: arc meridian three-sphere. Those two meridians are the
329: primitive dual-cell basis of \(H_3(B_C)\). It follows
330: integrally that
331: \[
332:                        \operatorname{im}\Delta
333:                               =\mathbb ZD_1\oplus\mathbb ZD_2.
334:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.12}
335: \]
336: 
337: Equivariance of \(F\) gives the equality of actual chain
338: maps
339: \[
340:              \operatorname{Var}_3\Delta
341:                          =\Delta\operatorname{Var}_2.
342:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.13}
343: \]
344: The target of \(\operatorname{Var}_2\) is \(H_2(E_C)=0\).
345: Thus (P3K.29.12) is contained in the kernel of the middle
346: variation. Symmetry then gives
347: \[
348:                        V=\operatorname{diag}(v,0,0).
349:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.14}
350: \]
351: Filling the other two curve tubes and using the actual
352: relative-disc map in Section 1 identifies \(v\) with the
353: single fixed-curve variation in the orientations
354: (P3K.29.2). These signs differ from the ordered product
355: disc in K23, as follows explicitly. In its first-resolution
356: coordinates, the complex orientation is
357: \(D^3_y\times S^3_q\), and the positive complex meridian
358: is \(m_0=S^3_q\). Thus
359: \[
360:       m_0\cdot[D^3_y]=(-1)^{3\cdot3}=-1,\qquad
361:       D_0=-[D^3_y].
362: \]
363: K23's product variation \(t_{\rm K23}\) is the coefficient
364: of \(S^3_q\) in \(F(D^3_y)-D^3_y\), and it equals
365: \(-k_{\rm Jin}\). Consequently
366: \[
367:                 v=-t_{\rm K23}=k_{\rm Jin}.
368: \]
369: Equivalently, the boundary of \(D_0\) has the complex
370: curve orientation, since \(y/|y|\) reverses that curve
371: orientation in K23. This is precisely the boundary-slope
372: orientation in (EVar.5), which gives the same integer.
373: For the primitive K22 families in K27,
374: \[
375:                               v=-3d.
376:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.15}
377: \]
378: 
379: ## 5. The matrix after the fixed-edge flop
380: 
381: The map \(F\) is the same common-exterior map, so its new
382: matrix is obtained by the two actual basis maps:
383: \[
384:  V'=M^{-1}VM^{-T}
385:    =v\begin{pmatrix}
386:        1&-2&-2\\
387:        -2&4&4\\
388:        -2&4&4
389:       \end{pmatrix}.                                   \tag{P3K.29.16}
390: \]
391: In particular the selected-edge variation remains \(v\),
392: and the two other diagonal entries become \(4v\).
393: These are variations of specified relative cycles.
394: They do not by themselves identify the relative stable
395: normal derivative of either of the latter edges.
396: 
397: For a flop of a nonfixed edge in a delta-retaining fan,
398: the relabelled matrix fixes the first meridian column.
399: Consequently the transformed variation is still
400: \(\operatorname{diag}(v,0,0)\). This follows by an exact
401: unimodular matrix calculation, not by discarding a cycle.
402: 
403: ## 6. Stable derivatives on the two original nonfixed cycles
404: 
405: There is also a direct integral vanishing for the original
406: two nonfixed cycles. Choose a stable frame of \(TB_C\)
407: by restricting a stable frame of the actual \(S^5\).
408: An invariant connection and the positive delta vector
409: give a stable frame of \(TE_C\). In this frame, the
410: derivative of the equivariant \(F\) is upper triangular:
411: it is the pullback of its quotient derivative on \(B_C\)
412: and the identity on the vertical line. The off-diagonal
413: connection term contracts linearly to zero, relative
414: to the boundary. Its stable derivative class is therefore
415: a pullback from \(B_C\).
416: 
417: The spaces are simply connected. The derivative and every
418: stable change of frame lift to the stable Spin group,
419: with the derivative equal to the identity on the boundary.
420: Its third homotopy generator is thus an integral
421: three-dimensional obstruction class, without a
422: two-to-one \(SO\)-Hurewicz ambiguity. A pullback class
423: evaluates to zero on the actual circle trace of a
424: two-cycle, because the composition of that trace with
425: \(\pi\) factors through the original two-cycle.
426: It therefore vanishes on \(D_1,D_2\).
427: 
428: This remains true in the single-curve product frame of
429: K23. Here is the exact frame-change formula. If
430: \(\sigma'=\sigma g\), then
431: \[
432:        c_{\sigma'}(F)(p)=
433:        g(Fp)^{-1}c_\sigma(F)(p)g(p).
434:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.17}
435: \]
436: The primitive third class of Spin, applied to a relative
437: three-cycle \(D\), changes by
438: \[
439:               -\langle g^*\eta,\operatorname{Var}_F(D)\rangle.
440:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.18}
441: \]
442: To verify this formula integrally, make \(g\) cellular and
443: constant on the two-skeleton, using the two-connectedness
444: of Spin. The product obstruction on each three-cell is
445: the sum of the three obstruction integers in
446: (P3K.29.17). The boundary terms cancel because \(F\) is
447: the identity there, leaving precisely the closed chain
448: \(F(D)-D\) with the minus sign in (P3K.29.18).
449: 
450: For \(D_1,D_2\), that variation is zero by
451: (P3K.29.14), so their stable derivative is zero in any
452: stable frame that extends to their single-curve exterior.
453: Choose the actual relative disc in Section 1. It misses
454: the other tubes, where the return is the identity, so the
455: same derivative evaluation, with the sign \(D_i=-[D^3_y]\)
456: just computed, is the negative of K23's full relative
457: single-curve derivative. Its value is therefore
458: \(+2n_{\rm rel}\), since K23 uses \(-2n_{\rm rel}\) on
459: the ordered product disc. The vanishing proves
460: \[
461:          n_{\rm rel}(C_1)=n_{\rm rel}(C_2)=0
462:              \quad\hbox{in a delta-retaining fan}.
463:                                                         \tag{P3K.29.19}
464: \]
465: This is an integer assertion in the specified actual
466: relative framing. It provides a second direct proof that
467: a nonfixed-edge flop in this fan sector leaves its
468: oriented homotopy-sphere class unchanged.
469: 
470: ## 7. Retained Pontryagin comparison
